\if{html}{\out{<a id="method-Ggplot2PolygonLayerProcessor-layer_polygon_grobs"></a>}}
\if{latex}{\out{\hypertarget{method-Ggplot2PolygonLayerProcessor-layer_polygon_grobs}{}}}
\subsection{\code{Ggplot2PolygonLayerProcessor$layer_polygon_grobs()}}{
  Panel polygons that a polygon layer could have drawn

The skip list is not defensive. \code{geom_boxplot()} draws each box's
crossbar through \code{GeomPolygon}, so a boxplot contributes grobs named
exactly like a polygon layer's own -- and they are drawn first.
Measured on three boxes beside one polygon:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{geom_boxplot.gTree.30
  geom_boxplot.gTree.10 -> geom_crossbar.gTree.9 -> geom_polygon.polygon.7
  geom_boxplot.gTree.20 -> geom_crossbar.gTree.19 -> geom_polygon.polygon.17
  geom_boxplot.gTree.28 -> geom_crossbar.gTree.27 -> geom_polygon.polygon.25
geom_polygon.polygon.32                                  <- the layer's own
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Taking the first match would outline a box. Every one of those sits
inside a tree named after the geom that owns it, so refusing to
descend into another layer's tree leaves exactly the layer's own --
which is how \code{layer_polyline_grobs()} scopes the same search for the
geoms that draw anonymous polylines.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{Ggplot2PolygonLayerProcessor$layer_polygon_grobs(
  plot,
  panel_grob,
  target = NULL
)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{plot}}{The ggplot2 object}
      \item{\code{panel_grob}}{The panel's grob tree}
      \item{\code{target}}{Index of the layer whose polygons are wanted}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    List of grobs in draw order
  }
}

